    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a> united the Massylii and Masaesyli after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-zama">Zama</a> in 202 BCE, creating a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> kingdom centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a>.</li><li>Its light cavalry was famed across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and Punic was the language of its court and towns.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurtha">Jugurtha</a>'s war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> (112–105 BCE) exposed Roman corruption, and Caesar annexed the kingdom after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thapsus">Thapsus</a> in 46 BCE.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sallust">Sallust</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurthine-war">Jugurthine War</a> is the main account of Numidia, was the first governor of the Roman province that replaced the kingdom.</p></aside>
    </section>

