    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/syphax"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/syphax.jpg" alt="Syphax" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/syphax">Syphax</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Syphax.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the late third century BCE the lands west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> were held by two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> peoples, the Massylii in the east and the larger Masaesyli in the west, whose king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syphax">Syphax</a> ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siga">Siga</a> and later from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a>, son of the Massylian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaia-numidian-king">Gaia</a>, was raised partly at Carthage and fought for the Carthaginians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, but in 206 BCE he went over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and its general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Publius Cornelius Scipio</a>. Driven from his inheritance by Syphax, who had allied himself with Carthage by marrying the Carthaginian noblewoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophonisba">Sophonisba</a>, Masinissa joined Scipio's invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. In 203 BCE he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-laelius">Gaius Laelius</a> defeated and captured Syphax, and Masinissa occupied Cirta; Sophonisba, whom he married, took poison rather than be led to Rome. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-zama">Battle of Zama</a> in 202 BCE Masinissa's cavalry helped decide Rome's victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>, and the peace of 201 BCE confirmed him as king of both Massylian and Masaesylian lands. For the first time eastern and western Numidia formed one kingdom, stretching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moulouya-river">Mulucha</a> (Moulouya) river to the borders of Carthaginian territory, with its capital at Cirta (modern Constantine). The treaty also forbade Carthage to make war without Roman consent, which left Masinissa free to press against it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rome"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rome.jpg" alt="Rome" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trevi_Fountain,_Rome,_Italy_2_-_May_2007.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diliff, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Numidia combined settled farming in the fertile plains and valleys of the east with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pastoralism">pastoralism</a> in the uplands and steppe. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> credits <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a> with turning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numidians">Numidians</a> into farmers, an exaggeration, since agriculture was already old there, but under him and his successors grain production grew and Numidian wheat was exported: shipments reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and Masinissa was honoured with statues on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delos">Delos</a>. The kingdom's towns, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dougga">Thugga</a> (Dougga), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zama-regia">Zama Regia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulla-regia">Bulla Regia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicca-veneria">Sicca</a>, had strong Punic influence, and Punic was the main written language of court and administration, alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyco-berber-script">Libyco-Berber script</a>, used in the bilingual dedication to Masinissa set up at Thugga in 139 BCE. Kings struck bronze coins with royal portraits and Punic legends. Religion blended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> cults with Punic gods such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal-hammon">Baal Hammon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanit">Tanit</a>, and the kings themselves received cult after death. Monumental tombs such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medracen">Medracen</a>, probably of the third century BCE, and the tower mausoleum at Thugga mixed local forms with Punic and Hellenistic architecture. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> fell in 146 BCE Rome gave the libraries of the city to the Numidian princes, and Italian merchants settled in Numidian towns, above all at Cirta.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/third-punic-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/third-punic-war.jpg" alt="Third Punic War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/third-punic-war">Third Punic War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:City_of_Carthage_circa_149_BC.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Harrias, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Numidian power rested on light cavalry, famous across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>: riders on small, hardy horses, controlled without bit or saddle, who fought with javelins and small shields, harassed enemies and pursued the defeated. Numidian horsemen served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a> brought them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s side. Masinissa also built up infantry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> and fought into extreme old age. Over half a century he took Carthaginian land piece by piece, including the coastal Emporia around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leptis-minor">Leptis Minor</a> around 161 BCE and the fertile Great Plains and the district of Tusca in the 150s; Roman envoys sent to judge these disputes generally favoured him. In 150 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> finally fought back and was defeated, and its breach of the treaty gave Rome its pretext for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-punic-war">Third Punic War</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bellum-iugurthinum">The Jugurthine War</a> of 112–105 BCE showed the strengths of Numidian warfare: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurtha">Jugurtha</a> bribed Roman commanders, forced an army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aulus-postumius-albinus">Aulus Postumius Albinus</a> to pass under the yoke near Suthul in 110 BCE, fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-numidicus">Quintus Caecilius Metellus</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-muthul">Battle of the Muthul</a> in 109 BCE and then turned to raids and mobile war in the interior. In 49 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-i">Juba I</a> destroyed the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-scribonius-curio-tribune-50-bce">Gaius Scribonius Curio</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-bagradas-river-49-bce">Battle of the Bagradas River</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/masinissa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/masinissa.jpg" alt="Masinissa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Masinissa_-_Capitoline_Museum.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Numidian monarchy was personal and dynastic, rooted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> kinship and chiefdoms, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a> and his heirs adopted the outward forms of Hellenistic kingship: a royal court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a>, coinage, royal titles in Punic and Greek, and ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and the Greek world. Succession among Berber rulers had traditionally passed to the eldest male of the royal kin, but Masinissa's long reign established the rule of his own descendants. On his death in 148 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus">Scipio Aemilianus</a>, acting for Rome, divided royal functions among his three legitimate sons: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/micipsa">Micipsa</a> held Cirta and the government, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulussa">Gulussa</a> the army and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mastanabal">Mastanabal</a> justice. Micipsa outlived his brothers and ruled alone until 118 BCE, adopting his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurtha">Jugurtha</a>, who had served with distinction under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio</a> Aemilianus at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numantia">Numantia</a>. Micipsa left the kingdom jointly to his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adherbal">Adherbal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiempsal-i">Hiempsal</a> and to Jugurtha, a settlement that collapsed when Jugurtha had Hiempsal killed. A Roman commission then divided Numidia in about 116 BCE, giving Jugurtha the west and Adherbal the east with Cirta. Administration below the king relied on local chiefs and town councils of Punic type with officials called sufetes, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a> increasingly acted as arbiter of disputed successions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bellum-iugurthinum"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bellum-iugurthinum.jpg" alt="Bellum Iugurthinum" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bellum-iugurthinum">Bellum Iugurthinum</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sulla_Coin2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Numidia was the first large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> state known in detail, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a> became a lasting symbol of North African kingship; modern Algerian and wider Amazigh historical memory claims him and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurtha">Jugurtha</a> as national figures. The kingdom spread Punic language, urban life and religion far into the interior after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> itself had fallen, so that Punic was still spoken in the region centuries later. Its role in Roman history was large. Masinissa's pressure on Carthage helped bring about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-punic-war">Third Punic War</a> and the destruction of Carthage in 146 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bellum-iugurthinum">The Jugurthine War</a> exposed corruption in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a>, gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius">Gaius Marius</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a> of 107 BCE, during which he enrolled men without property in the legions, and launched the career of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Lucius Cornelius Sulla</a>. It also produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sallust">Sallust</a>'s <em>Bellum Iugurthinum</em>, written by the first governor of the Roman province that replaced the kingdom and one of the classics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> historical writing, which drew partly on Punic books attributed to a King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiempsal-i">Hiempsal</a>, probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiempsal-ii">Hiempsal II</a>. Numidian cavalry continued to serve in Roman armies, and Numidian towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dougga">Thugga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulla-regia">Bulla Regia</a> grew into prosperous Roman cities whose temples, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> and inscriptions dominate the archaeology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thapsus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-thapsus.jpg" alt="Battle of Thapsus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thapsus">Battle of Thapsus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Thapsus,_46_BC_(Initial_deployment_of_troops).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The killing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adherbal">Adherbal</a> and of the Italian traders who had helped defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a> in 112 BCE brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> into open war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurtha">Jugurtha</a>. After years of fighting under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-creticus">Metellus</a> and then Marius, Jugurtha fled to his father-in-law, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bocchus-i">Bocchus I</a> of the Kingdom of Mauretania, who in 105 BCE handed him over to Marius's quaestor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>. Jugurtha was led in Marius's triumph in 104 BCE and died in prison in Rome. Rome gave much of the western kingdom to Bocchus and left the rest to Jugurtha's half-brother Gauda as a client king. Numidia now survived on Roman favour and became entangled in Rome's civil wars: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> invaded in 81 BCE, killed the king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiarbas">Hiarbas</a>, who had been installed by the Marian faction, and restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiempsal-ii">Hiempsal II</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiempsal-i">Hiempsal</a>'s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-i">Juba I</a> backed Pompey and the Senate against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, and after the death of Pompey he joined the republican leaders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thapsus">Battle of Thapsus</a> in 46 BCE Caesar defeated them, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-ii">Juba</a>, refused entry to his own town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-zama">Zama</a>, died in a suicide pact with the Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-petreius">Marcus Petreius</a>. Caesar made the core of the kingdom the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa-nova">Africa Nova</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sallust">Sallust</a> as its first governor, while land around Cirta went to his ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-sittius">Publius Sittius</a>. Juba's son, Juba II, was raised in Rome and later became king of Mauretania.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Numidia, united by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>, was the first great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> kingdom, spreading Punic urban culture inland, helping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> destroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and then, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurtha">Jugurtha</a>, fighting a war that exposed the corruption of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> before its absorption into Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in 46 BCE.</em></p>

